Exercises 7-9 fused the connective "where" into the transformation's math
span with the following variable ("$x=u^{2},y=v^{2},\text{where}\,S$ is the
rectangle..."), unlike exercise 10 a few lines later ("...$T(u,v)=(2u-v,u)$,
where $S$ is the triangle...") and the "where $a,b>0$"-style constructions
used throughout the rest of the file. Hand-fixed to close the math span
before "where" and open a fresh one for $S$, matching the file's own
established convention.

Exercises 21 and 22 (transformations into R^3, with x=u/a,y=v/b,z=w/c and
x=au,y=bv,z=cw respectively) defined the target region as
$R=\{(x,y)|...\}$ even though the set-builder condition involves x, y, and
z. Hand-fixed both to $R=\{(x,y,z)|...\}$. Confirmed against the live
OpenStax page that both instances of the two-variable set-builder header
are present verbatim in OpenStax's own source, not introduced by
conversion.

The figure caption before exercises 35-36 described the parallelogram R
(vertices (0,0),(1,0),(2,1),(1,1)) as a "rhombus." Its side lengths are 1,
sqrt(2), 1, sqrt(2) -- not equal, so it is a parallelogram but not a
rhombus, consistent with how the surrounding prose already names it.
Confirmed this mislabeling is present verbatim in OpenStax's own figure
caption. Hand-fixed to "parallelogram."

Exercise 40 wrapped the entire $\underset{R}{\iint}(x^{2}+25y^{2})$
construct in braces before applying the outer exponent 2
("${\underset{R}{\iint}(x^{2}+25y^{2})}^{2}\,dA$"), squaring the integral
sign itself rather than the integrand. Confirmed against the live OpenStax
page that the intended integrand is $(x^{2}+25y^{2})^{2}$. Hand-fixed to
$\underset{R}{\iint}{(x^{2}+25y^{2})}^{2}\,dA$, matching the
brace-then-exponent convention used elsewhere in the file for exponentiated
parenthetical quantities.

Exercise 45 asked to show
$\iint_R f(\sqrt{x^{2}/3+y^{2}/3})\,dA=2\pi\sqrt{15}\int_0^1 f(\rho)\rho\,d\rho$
for R bounded by the ellipse $5x^{2}+3y^{2}=15$ (i.e. $x^{2}/3+y^{2}/5=1$).
Substituting $x=\sqrt3\,\rho\cos\theta,y=\sqrt5\,\rho\sin\theta$ (Jacobian
$\sqrt{15}\,\rho$) only reduces the integrand to $f(\rho)$ and reproduces
the stated result when the second denominator is 5, not 3; with two
denominators of 3 the claimed identity does not hold. Confirmed against the
live OpenStax page that the source integrand is
$\sqrt{x^{2}/3+y^{2}/5}$. Hand-fixed the second denominator from 3 to 5.

Exercise 59's introductory sentence for Lamé ovals gave the variable list
"where a, b, and n are positive real numbers" in plain prose, unlike every
other "where <vars> are/is ..." construction in this file, which keeps the
variables in math mode (e.g. exercise 21's "where $a,b,c>0$"). Hand-fixed
to "where $a,b,\text{and}\,n$ are positive real numbers", matching the
file's own $\text{and}\,$ convention for a math-mode variable list ending
in "and".
